WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.154
Every model has practical token budgets.

00:00:02.154 --> 00:00:05.235
Input tokens include instructions, source material, conversation history, examples,

00:00:05.235 --> 00:00:06.947
retrieved documents, and tool outputs.

00:00:06.947 --> 00:00:09.685
Output tokens are the answer the model generates.

00:00:09.685 --> 00:00:12.424
A request has to leave room for both.

00:00:12.424 --> 00:00:17.216
If the input is too large, the system may reject the request, truncate material,

00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:21.324
or leave too little space for the response the user actually needs.

00:00:21.324 --> 00:00:23.720
Hosted models often tie tokens to cost.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.513
Sending a large body of text may cost more than sending a focused prompt,

00:00:28.513 --> 00:00:31.936
and asking for a long response increases the total further.

00:00:31.936 --> 00:00:36.044
Local models have different economics, but they still pay in RAM, VRAM,

00:00:36.044 --> 00:00:37.071
and processing time.

00:00:37.071 --> 00:00:41.179
Large contexts can increase prompt processing time and memory pressure even when

00:00:41.179 --> 00:00:42.891
there is no per-token bill.

00:00:42.891 --> 00:00:46.656
Latency matters because long context changes the feel of a workflow.

00:00:46.656 --> 00:00:51.449
A model may take longer to read the prompt before generating the first token.

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:55.214
Generation time may also increase if the requested output is long.

00:00:55.214 --> 00:00:56.241
For local models,

00:00:56.241 --> 00:01:00.349
the selected context length can affect how much memory is reserved and

00:01:00.349 --> 00:01:02.061
how stable the runtime feels.

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:05.142
Maximum context is not always the best operating point.

00:01:05.142 --> 00:01:08.223
Good context management balances performance, quality, and workload needs.

00:01:08.223 --> 00:01:13.015
If the task requires a full source set, the extra cost may be justified.

00:01:13.015 --> 00:01:17.808
If the task needs only a focused answer, a smaller context may be better.

00:01:17.808 --> 00:01:22.258
Engineers should treat context length as a tuning parameter, not a trophy number.

00:01:22.258 --> 00:01:27.393
The right amount of context is the amount that improves the outcome without unnecessary cost,

00:01:27.393 --> 00:01:28.420
latency, or noise.
